#!/usr/bin/python3
"""agentctl: runs Buzz agents on this machine for the Black 4 hardware provider.

Reads one JSON request on stdin, writes one JSON reply on stdout. The Buzz
plugin calls it over SSH. Each agent is a folder under ~/workforce/agents/<pubkey>
holding its key (mode 600), settings and log (agent.log, cut over to
agent.log.1 past 5 MB), and one buzz-acp process.

Requests: {"op": ...} with op one of
  provision, activate, profile, instructions.get, instructions.set,
  settings.set (model, effort, pays, compactAt, respondTo and businessId
               (see "whom an agent answers"), and buzz-acp's own levers:
               agents, sessionPolicy, multipleEventHandling, maxTurnsPerSession,
               maxTurnDuration, contextMessageLimit), remove, start, status, list   (one agent)
  identity                                (each agent's key and the owner it attests)
  machine.status                          (what this machine can run, and each
                                           harness's subscription and key sign-in)
  models                                  (the models and efforts each harness
                                           offers on each way of paying, and the
                                           defaults here)
  doctor                                  (machine.status plus every check that
                                           an agent can run now; `agentctl doctor`
                                           prints it, deploy.sh runs that)
  login.start, login.finish, login.cancel, logout   (a harness's subscription
                                           sign-in, or with pays "key" its API key)

Not requests (run from cron or by agentctl itself):
  agentctl supervise          keeps activated agents running; cron starts it
  agentctl list               prints each agent and whom it answers
  agentctl audience <pubkey>  asks Platform Control for an agent's audience again
  agentctl dropped <pubkey>   handles the mentions an agent's gate dropped (see
                              "a mention the gate drops")
  agentctl tool-gate          a customer-facing agent's Claude Code hook (see
                              "the customer-facing profile")
  agentctl login-helper <id>  holds one sign-in open between SSH calls
  agentctl check <slot>       checks a stored sign-in, out of band for machine.status
                              (a slot is a harness, or <harness>-key for its key)
  agentctl models <slot>      makes a slot's model listing again, out of band
  agentctl restart-agents <harness> [pays]   restarts agents after a sign-in
  agentctl apply-later <pubkey>   applies an agent's own change once its turn ends
  agentctl log-filter <raw> <log> <pid> <from>   follows buzz-acp's raw log into agent.log (every agent has one)

Callers: a teammate reaches this machine with their own SSH key, whose
authorized_keys entry forces `agentctl --caller <label>` (machine/teammates.sh
writes it), or through the hardware door (machine/door.mjs), which runs
`agentctl --caller <staff email>` for a request carrying a valid Platform
Control pass. The label or email is stored as each agent's creator (`createdBy`, shown in
`list`). A caller reads and changes every agent (they are the whole staff's), removes
only agents it created, and signs in and out only its own accounts. No label (the machine
owner's own unrestricted key, or cron) may do everything, and only it changes
the agents it made or ones made before creators were recorded.

Secrets: agent keys live in agents/<pubkey>/key. Every sign-in is one
person's: under accounts/<creator>/ (the caller's for login, logout,
machine.status and models; the agent's createdBy for everything about one
agent). Nothing falls back to another person's, and the admin has none.
`agentctl adopt-signins <email>` moves sign-ins made before this to one person
once. Within an account: the subscription sign-ins in
secrets/claude (the Claude token), homes/codex/auth.json and
homes/grok/auth.json (each CLI's own file, in a private home passed as
CODEX_HOME / GROK_HOME) and homes/qwen/subscription_credentials.json (Qwen
Code's own membership file, in QWEN_HOME); and each harness's API key in
secrets/<harness>-key (secrets/<harness>-key.json says which plan and base URL
it is for, never the key); Codex also keeps its own copy of its key in
homes/codex-key/auth.json, written by `codex login --with-api-key`, and the
two go together. None is ever printed, logged or put in a reply, and none
goes into a command line.

Root: ~/workforce, or BLACK4_WORKFORCE_ROOT for a second runner beside it (its
own agents, sign-ins and cron lines).

An agent changing itself: each agent's home holds a note on what it is and how
to read and change its own heartbeat, model, effort, instructions and restart
with agentctl from its shell. Such a request (from inside the agent's own
process tree) is saved at once and applied after its current turn, by a
detached waiter, so the turn that asked still finishes; see "an agent changing
itself" below. Requests from people restart the agent at once.

Black 4 MCP: when setup.sh has installed the connector (mcp/black4), every agent
gets it as buzz-acp's MCP server. It signs each request with the agent's own key
and owner attestation, so there is nothing to sign in to and no per-agent setting.
"""
